WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.490
Depending on which
archaeologist you talk to,

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.650
the Icknield Way is described either

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.390
as the oldest road in
Britain, or more carefully,

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:15.010
as one of Britain's oldest
still-surviving paths.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.710
It stretches more than 100 miles
across southeast England,

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.950
and there have been people
walking parts of this path

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.410
since before recorded history.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.770
Parts of it are still in common use.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.470
I drove here on the Icknield Way
for a couple of miles,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.800
it was just a regular country road.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.670
But parts of it were nearly lost forever.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.010
All over the English countryside,

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.420
you'll signs that say "public footpath",

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.600
or if you're also allowed to
ride a horse on it, "public bridleway".

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.280
These are formal rights
of way, protected by law,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.360
and there are more than 200,000 kilometres
of them across the country.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.890
Landowners do not get a say in
who walks along those paths

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.720
and it's a criminal offence to block them.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.350
The trouble is that some of
those paths have been lost.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.590
Sure, they might have
existed once,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.330
historically, the public had
a right of way,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.440
but they're not marked down anymore.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.340
There's meant to be a definitive map,
but it's not complete.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.770
It's almost like the landowners at the
time it was drawn up

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.940
might've had a say
in what was written down.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.450
- Back in the '50s and '60s was
when the legislation was introduced

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.540
to actually map public rights of way
for the first time.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.470
But it was done on a local level,

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.960
so there were definitely
inconsistencies across areas.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.370
People understood the guidance differently
or even in some small areas,

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.700
there might have been local opposition

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.880
that meant rights of way weren't recorded
that should have been.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.260
So we've got an incomplete network.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.350
- Despite the Icknield Way
being thousands of years old,

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.230
legally opening every last bit of it
to the public took a lot of effort.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:46.960
Not just research, but also some fairly militant
walking enthusiasts turning up at public inquiries.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.570
This bit I'm walking on wasn't
marked on maps until the 1980s.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.370
As the 21st century approached,
the government agreed

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:59.600
that having these maybe-paths all over
the country might be a bad idea.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.490
- In the year 2000, the
Countryside and Rights of Way Act

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.610
opened large areas of country for
open access for the first time,

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:10.000
but at the same time,
landowners wanted some certainty

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.470
about the rights of way map.
If they bought a piece of land,

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.330
if there was a right
of way on there or not.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:18.170
So as part of the negotiation,
there was a cutoff date introduced

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.250
and that was the 1st of January 2026,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.400
and that was basically 25 years on
from that legislation,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.010
so we've got, you know,
just under five years

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.890
to find all these paths and
put them back on the map.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.240
- So now, under law, England
has until January 1st, 2026

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.450
to find and log all its footpaths.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.550
And as with any project with a deadline
that's a long way away,

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.660
very little has happened
over the last couple of decades.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.710
But with five years to go,
the Ramblers charity decided

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.970
to try and solve the problem
using very modern tools.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.130
They asked thousands of people
to check one bit of the map each.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.120
- They were shown that square
on the current OS map

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.160
and they were shown that square
on a couple of historic maps

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.880
from about 120 years ago,
and then we basically got people

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.720
to draw on the difference,
do a big spot the difference.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.340
We think we've found tens of thousands
of miles of lost rights of way.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.000
This isn't about creating
new rights of way,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.560
it's about recording ones that should've
been recorded in the first place.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.269
Most landowners understand
the rights of way network

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:20.840
brings billions of pounds
to the economy each year.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.960
We find a lot of farmers
and landowners like

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.130
to use that public rights
of way network as well.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.180
- That is a very... political answer.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.210
There are certainly stories
in the last few decades

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.830
of landowners intimidating or
bribing locals or officials

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.610
into just quietly
forgetting about some paths.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.720
There are plenty of people

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:41.120
who would very much like the
public to "get off their land",

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.196
and suddenly finding out that there's
a right of way through your farm

00:03:44.196 --> 00:03:46.530
is often not going to be appreciated.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:50.560
- They can only really object on the basis
of the historical evidence.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.290
They can't object on the basis that

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.140
they don't want that path or
that path is inconvenient,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.040
but we really want to
work with landowners.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.770
There's no point, from
a walker's perspective,

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.640
a path just going
through somebody's garden

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:02.840
if there's a way round that.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.530
So, you know, in some ways,
the real work starts now

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.730
and that's about proving that

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.260
these paths were historically
public rights of way

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.920
and actually submitting
them to local authorities

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.730
so they can be put back on the map.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.080
- England is approaching a point
that's very rare in history.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.980
It started with the first
concept of owning land,

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.980
which was absolutely not some
human universal constant.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.470
Then, through the feudal system
and the idea

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.970
of all land ultimately
belonging to the monarch.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.110
Then, to private landowners and
folk knowledge of footpaths.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:34.740
After all, when most people

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.950
never went more than a few miles
from home for their whole lives,

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.620
why would you need a map of the footpaths?

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:40.470
Everyone knew what they were,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.920
they were common knowledge passed down
from generation to generation.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.660
But now we're in an age
of GPS, of precise maps,

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.260
and of the expectation that everything
will be on some database somewhere.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:55.527
The process of defining who
has the right to walk where in England

00:04:55.527 --> 00:04:59.600
has been going on for millennia,
since before written history,

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.760
but it ends soon, January 1st 2026.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.670
After that, the definitive map is set,

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.970
and there are no mysterious
maybe-footpaths out there.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.600
If the Icknield Way hadn't been marked,
it could've been forgotten about.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.960
Either it exists on the map
or it's gone forever.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.450
It's not often that you see
a millennia-long process

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.357
come to an end.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:24.980
Thank you to Jack Cornish
from the Ramblers

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.610
and everyone who helped with my research.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.893
I've put links to my
sources in the description.

